FAQS = [["What is the best AI image generator in 2026?","There is no verified universal winner. GPT Image 2.5 offers an API-oriented image creation and editing workflow, Midjourney V8.2 is a subscription-first creative environment, Nano Banana 2.1 supports multi-turn and reference-driven editing, FLUX offers API and selected open-weight workflows, and Ideogram 4.5 provides generation and precise editing. Test your own prompts and permissions."],["Which AI image generator is best for text in images?","Ideogram specializes in typography and design, while Google describes improved text rendering for Nano Banana 2.1. Use an exact-lettering test at the required size; no unmeasured winner is claimed."],["What does GPT Image 2.5 cost?","OpenAI lists Standard GPT Image 2.5 image output at $30 per million image output tokens, image input at $8 per million and text input at $5 per million. Cost per image varies with output tokens, size and quality; use the current OpenAI image calculator."],["How much does Midjourney cost per month?","Midjourney lists Basic $10, Standard $30, Pro $60 and Mega $120 per month, with lower effective monthly prices when billed annually. Plans differ in GPU time, privacy and features."],["Does Midjourney have an official API?","Midjourney states that it generally does not provide a public API and prohibits unauthorized automation. Do not depend on third-party unofficial wrappers without checking its terms."],["What is Nano Banana 2.1?","Nano Banana 2.1 is Google's Gemini image model available through the Gemini API under the identifier gemini-nano-banana-2.1. It supports image creation, conversational editing and multiple reference inputs."],["Can I run FLUX locally?","Some FLUX model weights are available for local inference. FLUX.2 Klein 4B weights are Apache 2.0 according to the official repository; other FLUX variants have different licenses. FLUX 3 Image Klein offers downloadable weights, but commercial deployment needs a separate license."],["Can I use AI-generated images commercially?","Commercial rights are provider-, account-, model- and use-case-specific. Midjourney has a Pro/Mega requirement for businesses above its stated revenue threshold. Ideogram and Black Forest Labs distinguish hosted/API rights from self-hosted weight licenses. Verify current terms and third-party rights."],["Which image generator is cheapest?","A universal price winner cannot be inferred from mixed subscription, image, token and megapixel billing. Compare cost per accepted image with the same quality target, edits, retries, tool charges and license requirements."],["Is Ideogram better than Midjourney for logos?","Ideogram publishes typography and precise editing features, and Midjourney offers creative generation plus its V8.2 editing model. Exact text fidelity, brand consistency and legal trademark clearance must be tested rather than assumed."],["How can I compare AI image generators fairly?","Run the same documented prompt and input assets across eligible models, fix resolution/aspect ratio/seed where supported, generate multiple samples, blind-rate criteria and report costs, revisions, failure cases and date/version. SXF provides a repeatable protocol rather than fabricated benchmark results."],["Is open-weight image generation always free for commercial use?","No. Public weight downloads do not guarantee commercial rights. Different FLUX and Ideogram variants have Apache 2.0, non-commercial or separately licensed weight terms. Check the exact checkpoint and deployment license."]]
BODY_HTML = "<section class=\"shell image-buyers-guide\" data-image-buyers-guide aria-labelledby=\"image-buyers-heading\">\n  <div class=\"ibg-jump\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">THE SXF DECISION GUIDE</p><h2 id=\"image-buyers-heading\">Choose an AI image generator by the image you need to deliver.</h2><p>Searching for the <strong>best AI image generator</strong> in 2026? The answer changes with the job. A poster with exact words, a product photograph that must keep the same packaging, an editable ad, and a high-volume API pipeline have different requirements. This guide compares <strong>GPT Image, Midjourney, Nano Banana, FLUX and Ideogram</strong> using vendor-documented capabilities, public rates and a reproducible evaluation checklist—not an invented visual leaderboard.</p>\n  <nav aria-label=\"Guide sections\" class=\"ibg-toc\"><a href=\"#ibg-shortlist\">Quick picks</a><a href=\"#ibg-compare\">Comparison table</a><a href=\"#ibg-prices\">2026 prices</a><a href=\"#ibg-models\">Five platforms</a><a href=\"#ibg-tests\">Original test protocol</a><a href=\"#ibg-licenses\">Commercial rights</a><a href=\"#ibg-faq\">FAQ</a></nav>\n  <p class=\"ibg-source-note\">Source verification: <time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">October 31, 2026</time>. All prices are US dollars unless stated. Product capabilities and terms can change; this is a fact-checked buying framework, not a claim that SXF has generated or visually scored output from all five systems.</p></div>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-shortlist\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">DIRECT ANSWER / SEARCH INTENT</p><h2>Which AI image generator should you try first?</h2>\n<div class=\"ibg-quick-grid\"><article><span>PRECISE IMAGE EDITS</span><h3>GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst</h3><p>OpenAI positions Sunburst for editing precision and multi-step image workflows using the Image and Responses APIs. Compare its edit fidelity on your real assets.</p><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-image-2.5-sunburst\">Official specification ↗</a></article><article><span>CREATIVE WEB WORKFLOW</span><h3>Midjourney V8.2</h3><p>Subscription-based generation with personalization and an integrated editor. Evaluate for concept art and creative direction, not unattended API automation.</p><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32199405667853-Version\">Version reference ↗</a></article><article><span>MULTI-REFERENCE &amp; EDITING</span><h3>Nano Banana 2.1</h3><p>Google documents conversational image editing, image references, up to 4K output and an API model identifier for application builders.</p><a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation\">Gemini guide ↗</a></article><article><span>SELF-HOSTED PIPELINES</span><h3>FLUX family</h3><p>Black Forest Labs offers both hosted image models and weight releases, with license terms that differ by checkpoint. Start with licensing and hardware.</p><a href=\"https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux2\">FLUX.2 source ↗</a></article><article><span>TEXT-HEAVY DESIGN</span><h3>Ideogram 4.5</h3><p>Ideogram offers generation and precise image editing and documents workflows aimed at typography and design. Validate the actual wording before production.</p><a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/models/4.5\">Ideogram 4.5 ↗</a></article></div>\n<blockquote class=\"ibg-answer\"><strong>SXF answer:</strong> There is no defensible all-purpose “best” image generator without a shared test set. Choose a candidate by required output, editing tools, licensing and cost per <em>accepted</em> image, then validate against identical tasks.</blockquote></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-compare\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">FEATURE MATRIX</p><h2>GPT Image vs Midjourney vs Nano Banana vs FLUX vs Ideogram: comparison.</h2>\n<p>The table separates each company's <strong>product interface</strong> from a particular <strong>model version</strong>. “Documented” does not mean a model was measured against competitors. Choose the exact currently available version when testing.</p>\n<div class=\"ibg-scroll\"><table class=\"ibg-table\"><caption>Provider-documented image generation options checked on October 31, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Platform / model</th><th scope=\"col\">Primary use to evaluate</th><th scope=\"col\">Image editing</th><th scope=\"col\">Official developer path</th><th scope=\"col\">Local weights</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">OpenAI — GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst / Flare</th><td>Precision edits / fast generation</td><td>Yes: Image and multi-turn Responses workflow</td><td>Image API + Responses API</td><td>No public self-hosted weights listed</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Midjourney — V8.2</th><td>Creative exploration and styling</td><td>Yes: its web Editor / Edit Model</td><td>Website; <strong>no general official public API</strong></td><td>No public weights</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Google — Nano Banana 2.1</th><td>Multi-turn image work, references, text and 1K–4K outputs</td><td>Yes</td><td>Gemini API and Google AI Studio</td><td>No public weights for this hosted model</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Black Forest Labs — FLUX.2 / FLUX 3 Klein</th><td>Commercial API imagery; some local development</td><td>Version dependent; documented for both families</td><td>BFL API and some downloadable checkpoints</td><td><strong>Yes, selected variants only</strong>; licenses differ</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Ideogram — 4.5</th><td>Typography, design assets and precise edits</td><td>Yes: Precise Edit endpoint</td><td>Ideogram API and web app</td><td>Separate Ideogram 4 weights/license path; not identical to 4.5 API</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p class=\"ibg-note\">Specific version matters. This is not a timeless statement that every FLUX version can be run commercially, or that the latest web app, API and open-weights model share one feature list.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-prices\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">VERIFIED COST SNAPSHOT</p><h2>AI image generator pricing in 2026: what does an image really cost?</h2><p>A subscription month, a generated image, image output tokens and a megapixel-based charge are <strong>different units</strong>. Do not place them in a single “cheapest” ranking without specifying usage. Rates below are selected public examples, not personalized quotes.</p>\n<div class=\"ibg-scroll\"><table class=\"ibg-table\"><caption>Official selected US pricing examples, checked October 31, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th>Product and meter</th><th>Published example</th><th>Important limit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th>Midjourney monthly subscription</th><td><strong>$10 / $30 / $60 / $120</strong> for Basic / Standard / Pro / Mega</td><td>GPU allowances differ; company revenue rule applies to commercial use</td><td><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/27870484040333-Comparing-Midjourney-Plans\">Plan comparison</a></td></tr>\n<tr><th>Google Nano Banana 2.1 API</th><td><strong>$0.0336</strong> indicative 1K image; <strong>$0.0504</strong> 2K; <strong>$0.113</strong> 4K</td><td>Official equivalent image-output estimate; additional prompt/input/grounding charges may apply</td><td><a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing\">Gemini pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><th>Ideogram 4.5 API</th><td><strong>$0.03 / $0.06 / $0.10</strong> per prompt-only image (Low / Medium / High)</td><td>Reference-image and precise-edit High variants may cost $0.22</td><td><a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/pricing/?pricing_tab=api\">Ideogram pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><th>BFL FLUX 3 Image 1 API</th><td><strong>$0.048</strong> at 1K; <strong>$0.10</strong> at 2K list rates</td><td>Historical promotional discounts ended October 8, 2026; 4K costs more</td><td><a href=\"https://bfl.ai/pricing\">BFL pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><th>OpenAI GPT Image 2.5 API</th><td><strong>$30 / 1M image output tokens</strong> (Standard), image input $8 / 1M, text input $5 / 1M</td><td>Actual cost per image depends on output tokens, quality, resolution and edit inputs</td><td><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation\">OpenAI image pricing guide</a></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Example budget check:</strong> 100 accepted Ideogram 4.5 Medium prompt-only images at $0.06 each have a $6.00 direct generation cost if every attempt is accepted. At 50% acceptance and one charged image per attempt, obtaining 100 accepted images requires about 200 attempts and $12.00 before other expenses. This arithmetic is illustrative, not a measured vendor acceptance rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Better metric:</strong> total spend ÷ accepted assets. Include unwanted regenerations, edit attempts, upscales, storage, tool fees, time spent correcting typography, and the required commercial license. For token-priced models, read actual API usage rather than assuming every image has a fixed output-token count.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-models\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">PRACTICAL PLATFORM DEEP DIVE</p><h2>Which image model is suitable for which real workflow?</h2>\n<h3>1. GPT Image 2.5: precise edits, marketing visuals and application integration</h3><p>OpenAI documents two current 2.5 variants: <strong>Sunburst</strong>, positioned for editing precision, and <strong>Flare</strong>, positioned for fast everyday generation. The Image API handles individual generation or edit requests; the Responses API adds multi-turn image work inside a conversation. Both models have token-based pricing and support image and text inputs. Do not treat the older GPT Image 2 per-image illustration as a universal price for the newer 2.5 variants.</p><p><strong>Where to test it:</strong> changing one product's background without altering a label, revising a banner while preserving layout, or producing multiple ratio variants for an ad. Validate exact lettering, reference fidelity, crop safety and whether regeneration changes unintended details. <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation\">Official API guide</a> · <a href=\"/models/gpt-image-2/\">GPT Image 2 model entry</a>.</p>\n<h3>2. Midjourney V8.2: creative direction and iteration in a subscription</h3><p>Midjourney describes <strong>V8.2</strong> as its default version from July 24, 2026, including an updated Edit Model. Its web editor supports operations such as inpainting, outpainting, remixing and modifications of user-supplied images. Midjourney's plans are subscription/GPU-time products, not direct equivalents of pay-per-image APIs.</p><p><strong>Important for developers:</strong> the company's community guidelines say unauthorized automation and third-party integrations are not permitted, and that it generally does not offer a public API. If you require a production endpoint with documented service integration, evaluate an official API product rather than an unofficial Midjourney wrapper. For commercial work, review its revenue-based plan condition. <a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32199405667853-Version\">Version information</a> · <a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32013696484109-Community-Guidelines\">Automation policy</a>.</p>\n<h3>3. Google Nano Banana 2.1: references, multi-turn consistency and 4K output</h3><p>Google calls <strong>gemini-nano-banana-2.1</strong> its primary efficient image-generation and conversational editing model. Its guide describes support for image references, text rendering, and 1K, 2K and 4K output options, with documented limits depending on the variant. Google reports SynthID watermarks on generated Nano Banana images; do not equate this with an infallible detector for every image on the internet.</p><p><strong>Where to test it:</strong> generating a small campaign with recurring characters or products, editing an existing asset over several turns, and checking an Arabic or English typographic layout. Some pricing examples apply specifically to output images and do not include all input or grounding costs. <a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation\">Google model documentation</a> · <a href=\"/models/gemini-nano-banana-2-1/\">SXF model reference</a>.</p>\n<h3>4. FLUX: FLUX.2 hosted API versus open weights and FLUX 3 Klein</h3><p>Black Forest Labs provides hosted generation and editing through its APIs, plus selected open-weight checkpoints. <strong>FLUX.2 Klein 4B</strong> is documented under Apache 2.0 in the official code repository, while the 9B and dev variants carry non-commercial conditions. <strong>FLUX 3 Image Klein</strong> is downloadable and can run on a consumer GPU, but BFL explicitly says commercial self-hosted deployment requires a commercial license. Neither label “FLUX” nor “open weights” is enough to determine commercial rights.</p><p><strong>Where to test it:</strong> local inference privacy, high-volume batch processing, production API cost, fine-tuning eligibility and multiple-reference edits. Match the exact checkpoint, hardware and license to your business. <a href=\"https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux2\">Official FLUX.2 repository</a> · <a href=\"/guides/flux-3-image-klein/\">SXF FLUX 3 Klein installation and licensing guide</a>.</p>\n<h3>5. Ideogram 4.5: posters, exact wording and precise edits</h3><p>Ideogram positions its image tooling around design, typography and editing. <strong>Ideogram 4.5</strong> documents separate Generate and Precise Edit API endpoints and multiple quality modes. Its published API table gives prompt-only 1K/2K generation prices from $0.03 to $0.10, with higher rates for certain reference-driven and precise edits.</p><p><strong>Where to test it:</strong> poster headlines, readable packaging mockups, logo concepts and localized marketing. Count spelling failures at final output size—no design model should be presumed to reproduce every string flawlessly. Ideogram's <strong>Ideogram 4 open weights</strong> have separate non-commercial and paid commercial self-hosting terms; do not confuse them with the hosted 4.5 API. <a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/models/4.5\">Official 4.5 guide</a> · <a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/licensing/\">Licensing options</a>.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-tests\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">ORIGINAL SXF EVALUATION DESIGN</p><h2>A reproducible five-task test instead of an invented ranking.</h2><p>These are **SXF-designed test prompts and acceptance criteria**, not claims that image generations were executed or visually reviewed. Use them to compare versions in your own environment. Keep the prompt language, output aspect ratio and reference material as consistent as the interfaces allow. Save all samples, not just the best one.</p>\n<div class=\"ibg-test-grid\"><article><span>TEST 01 / TYPOGRAPHY</span><h3>Exact text on a poster</h3><p class=\"ibg-prompt\">“Design a clean 4:5 conference poster. The exact headline must read ‘BUILD BETTER IMAGES’ and the small footer ‘LJUBLJANA 2026’. No other words. Modern high-contrast typography and ample empty space.”</p><p><strong>Pass:</strong> every required letter and numeral is correct and readable; no invented text.</p></article><article><span>TEST 02 / PRODUCT FIDELITY</span><h3>Keep one object recognizable</h3><p class=\"ibg-prompt\">“Create a realistic studio product photograph of an unbranded cobalt-blue reusable water bottle with a black cap and a single white vertical stripe, on a pale neutral background. Soft side lighting. No logo or text.”</p><p><strong>Pass:</strong> specified cap, color, stripe and shape survive; no unwanted labels.</p></article><article><span>TEST 03 / REFERENCE EDIT</span><h3>Change only the background</h3><p class=\"ibg-prompt\">“Using the supplied reference photo, replace only the background with a warm minimalist studio. Preserve the exact object silhouette, material, orientation, color and every visible label.”</p><p><strong>Pass:</strong> editable region changes; foreground, identity and text remain stable. Requires the same rights-cleared reference photo for every model.</p></article><article><span>TEST 04 / COMPOSITION</span><h3>Count and layout</h3><p class=\"ibg-prompt\">“Overhead photograph of exactly four distinct ceramic cups in a two-by-two grid: red top left, blue top right, green bottom left, yellow bottom right. No additional cups.”</p><p><strong>Pass:</strong> correct count, positions and colors without missing or duplicated items.</p></article><article><span>TEST 05 / PRODUCTION</span><h3>Multi-format campaign</h3><p class=\"ibg-prompt\">“Create the same product campaign concept in 1:1, 4:5 and 16:9 versions. Preserve the object, palette, headline and focal subject while adapting safe margins for each crop.”</p><p><strong>Pass:</strong> product consistency, legible content, and crop-safe layout across formats.</p></article></div>\n<h3>How to score fairly</h3><p>For each test, record <strong>model ID/version, date, prompt, uploaded reference checksum, target resolution, number of attempts, direct charges and an anonymized output ID</strong>. Run at least five independent samples per test; independent human reviewers should rate prompt adherence, text accuracy, reference consistency, artifacts, editing preservation and brand safety using a written 0–4 rubric. Keep price and latency separate from aesthetic scores so they cannot disguise failure to follow instructions.</p><div class=\"ibg-scroll\"><table class=\"ibg-table\"><caption>Blank comparison scorecard; not benchmark results</caption><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>What to measure</th><th>Accept threshold example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Text fidelity</th><td>Correct strings ÷ required strings</td><td>100% for brand headlines</td></tr><tr><th>Object consistency</th><td>Blind human rating (0–4)</td><td>≥3/4 on accepted assets</td></tr><tr><th>Instruction adherence</th><td>Passed required attributes ÷ total</td><td>≥90% for product mockups</td></tr><tr><th>Edit preservation</th><td>Changed region versus unchanged reference</td><td>No unintended changes to protected elements</td></tr><tr><th>Economics</th><td>Total generation + edit charges ÷ accepted images</td><td>Compare to your real budget</td></tr><tr><th>Delivery</th><td>p50 and p95 completion time</td><td>Measure with your network and account</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"ibg-note\">These thresholds are illustrative editorial requirements, not provider performance claims. Publish the raw examples, model settings and negative cases before announcing any comparative winner. Seed locking is not supported consistently across vendors, so a shared prompt does not imply identical random conditions.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-licenses\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">COMMERCIAL RIGHTS &amp; PROVENANCE</p><h2>Can you use AI images for ads, ecommerce and client work?</h2><p>“Commercial use” has at least three separate questions: (1) may you use the <strong>output</strong> under the provider's terms? (2) may you <strong>self-host or fine-tune the model weights</strong>? (3) can you legally use the <strong>underlying people, logos, designs and third-party assets</strong> depicted? A positive answer to one does not establish the others.</p>\n<div class=\"ibg-rights\"><article><h3>Midjourney</h3><p>Its published rules generally allow subscribers to use outputs commercially, but require Pro or Mega for businesses grossing more than $1 million per year. Read the actual terms, privacy modes and asset ownership exceptions.</p></article><article><h3>Ideogram</h3><p>Its hosted output policy differs from the Ideogram 4 open-weight license. Public quantized weights are under a non-commercial agreement; commercial self-hosted use needs an appropriate paid license.</p></article><article><h3>FLUX</h3><p>FLUX.2 Klein 4B weights and 9B/dev weights use different licenses. BFL requires commercial licensing for FLUX 3 Image Klein self-hosted deployment. Hosted API terms are another contract.</p></article><article><h3>Other hosted models</h3><p>Check OpenAI and Google terms governing the exact service and intended content, plus third-party rights, publicity, trademarks and privacy. An API's availability does not prove that a particular generated logo is clear for registration.</p></article></div><p>For deeper licensing distinctions see <a href=\"/guides/ai-model-licensing/\">SXF's AI model licensing guide</a>. For image provenance and detection limitations see <a href=\"/guides/how-to-tell-if-an-image-is-ai-generated/\">how to tell if an image is AI-generated</a>. Neither an image watermark nor a detector replaces source documentation.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SELECTION FRAMEWORK</p><h2>A six-step buying checklist for teams and solo creators.</h2><ol class=\"ibg-checklist\"><li><strong>Define the deliverable:</strong> commercial poster, catalog photo, game concept, social visual or API workflow.</li><li><strong>Pin the exact model:</strong> GPT Image 2.5, Midjourney V8.2, Nano Banana 2.1, FLUX checkpoint or Ideogram 4.5; never evaluate a brand name alone.</li><li><strong>Identify the integration:</strong> browser application, official API, plug-in or legally licensed local weights.</li><li><strong>Apply non-negotiables:</strong> exact text, face consent, reference fidelity, privacy requirements, required file dimensions and downstream edits.</li><li><strong>Run the same challenge set:</strong> use the five prompts above and preserve failures, not just attractive successes.</li><li><strong>Calculate cost per accepted output:</strong> include retries, hours, subscriptions, API charges, commercial licensing and human finishing time.</li></ol>\n<p>For low-risk exploration, start with the vendor's supported UI or sandbox. For a commercial pipeline, demand documented APIs, stable permissions, version pinning where supported, audit logs and a clearly reviewable license. An appealing gallery image is not evidence of production repeatability.</p></section>\n\n<section class=\"ibg-section\" id=\"ibg-faq\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SEARCH QUESTIONS ANSWERED</p><h2>Best AI image generator FAQ.</h2><div class=\"ibg-faq\">\n<details><summary>What is the best AI image generator for photorealistic product images?</summary><p>Shortlist OpenAI GPT Image, Google Nano Banana, FLUX and Ideogram, then use the same rights-cleared product reference and measure packaging fidelity, unwanted text, lighting quality and cost per usable output. No provider has a proven universal advantage from the evidence presented here.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Which AI image generator is best for text in images?</summary><p>Ideogram specializes in typography; Google documents improved text rendering for Nano Banana 2.1. Test exact headlines and small labels at final resolution. Do not replace quality control with a marketing promise.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is GPT Image better than Midjourney?</summary><p>They are different workflows: GPT Image offers documented developer APIs and conversational edits, while Midjourney V8.2 is a creative subscription tool with a browser editor and no general official public API. Select based on integration needs and tested outputs.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can Nano Banana 2.1 create 4K images?</summary><p>Google documents 1K, 2K and 4K output tiers for Nano Banana 2.1, subject to supported aspect ratios and API constraints.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How much does a Midjourney subscription cost?</summary><p>Current official monthly plans are $10 Basic, $30 Standard, $60 Pro and $120 Mega. Annual billing has a lower effective per-month price, and plan features differ.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does Midjourney offer an API for developers?</summary><p>Midjourney says it generally does not offer a public API and prohibits unauthorized automation. Use a provider with a documented API for production integrations.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can FLUX be run locally for free?</summary><p>Some checkpoints are downloadable, but the commercial license and hardware burden depend on the exact variant. FLUX.2 Klein 4B is Apache 2.0; FLUX.2 9B and dev are non-commercial, and FLUX 3 Image Klein commercial self-hosting needs a license.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How much does Ideogram 4.5 cost through the API?</summary><p>Ideogram lists prompt-only generation from $0.03 (Low) to $0.10 (High) per image, with higher prices for certain source-image edits. Recheck current API pricing before budgeting.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Are all AI-generated images legally safe to use commercially?</summary><p>No. Provider output permissions, weight licensing, model usage policies and third-party intellectual property or publicity rights must be evaluated separately.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What is the fairest AI image generator benchmark?</summary><p>A source-logged protocol with identical prompts, matching references, multiple samples, blind human ratings, actual cost per accepted output and published limitations. SXF provides this protocol without making untested rankings.</p></details>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"ibg-section\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">OFFICIAL SOURCE LEDGER</p><h2>References and verification method.</h2><p>Reviewed October 31, 2026. Sources are first-party model documentation, published pricing and legal terms, not affiliate roundup pages. Marketing claims and model positioning are labelled as such. SXF has not run a controlled provider benchmark for this article; the scorecard is a reproducible test design. Update the source prices and version names when providers change their live documentation.</p>\n<div class=\"ibg-source-list\"><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>OpenAI Image generation guide</strong><span>Model selection, image creation/editing, calculation methodology ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-image-2.5-sunburst\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>OpenAI GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst</strong><span>High-precision editing model and token billing ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-image-2.5-flare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>OpenAI GPT Image 2.5 Flare</strong><span>Fast generation model and token billing ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Google Gemini image generation</strong><span>Nano Banana 2.1 versions, resolutions and references ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Google Gemini API pricing</strong><span>Nano Banana 2.1 rates and indicative image costs ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32199405667853-Version\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Midjourney model versions</strong><span>V8.2 model and editing functionality ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/27870484040333-Comparing-Midjourney-Plans\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Midjourney subscriptions</strong><span>Current subscription pricing and plan limitations ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/32013696484109-Community-Guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Midjourney automation policy</strong><span>No generally available public automation API ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.midjourney.com/hc/en-us/articles/27870375276557-Using-Images-Videos-Commercially\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Midjourney commercial rights</strong><span>Company revenue threshold and image rights ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://bfl.ai/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Black Forest Labs image API pricing</strong><span>FLUX.2 and FLUX 3 image billing ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://bfl.ai/models/flux-3-image-klein\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Black Forest Labs FLUX 3 Klein</strong><span>Open weights, local deployment and commercial license ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Official FLUX.2 model repository</strong><span>Model-by-model open-weight license distinctions ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/pricing/?pricing_tab=api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Ideogram API pricing</strong><span>Ideogram 4.5 output-based API tiers ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/models/4.5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Ideogram 4.5 product</strong><span>Precise edit workflow and supported APIs ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://ideogram.ai/licensing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Ideogram weight licenses</strong><span>Self-hosted versus hosted license rights ↗</span></a></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"ibg-section\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">CONTINUE EXPLORING SXF / AI</p><h2>Related guides and model references.</h2><div class=\"model-related-links\">\n<a href=\"/guides/flux-3-image-klein/\"><span>LOCAL GENERATION</span><strong>FLUX 3 Image Klein: setup, VRAM and licensing</strong><b>↗</b></a>\n<a href=\"/guides/ai-model-licensing/\"><span>COMMERCIAL RIGHTS</span><strong>AI model licensing and compliance</strong><b>↗</b></a>\n<a href=\"/guides/how-to-tell-if-an-image-is-ai-generated/\"><span>PROVENANCE</span><strong>How to tell if an image is AI-generated</strong><b>↗</b></a>\n<a href=\"/models/\"><span>MODEL DIRECTORY</span><strong>Explore SXF's image model catalog</strong><b>↗</b></a>\n<a href=\"/guides/how-to-choose-ai-model/\"><span>DECISION FRAMEWORK</span><strong>How to choose an AI model</strong><b>↗</b></a>\n</div></section>\n</section>"

